🔍 Pipeline Architecture
Raw CSV Data
     ↓
MongoDB Atlas (NoSQL staging — raw, unstructured layer)
     ↓
Python ETL (cleaning, datetime parsing, feature engineering)
     ↓
PostgreSQL / Supabase (structured, query-ready layer)
     ↓
SQL Analysis + Statistical Testing
     ↓
Looker Studio Dashboard

📈 Key Insights
Peak Demand Window: Load consistently peaks between 7–9 PM, reflecting the evening residential demand surge as households return home and use appliances, lighting, and heating/cooling systems.
Weekday vs Weekend: Weekend load is statistically significantly higher than weekday load (independent t-test: p < 0.001), suggesting different usage patterns when residents are home for longer stretches.
Temperature Effect (Counter-Intuitive Finding): Rather than peaking in hot weather due to air conditioning, load demand was highest during colder periods — indicating that heating-related consumption may be a stronger driver of demand than cooling in this dataset.

📊 Dashboard
An interactive dashboard was built in Google Looker Studio featuring:
Hourly Load Distribution — identifying peak and off-peak hours
Temperature vs Load Correlation — visualizing the demand-temperature relationship
Weekday vs Weekend Comparison — highlighting behavioral differences in consumption
(Dashboard screenshots included below)

💡 Business Recommendation
Given the consistent 7–9 PM peak demand window — particularly pronounced during colder months — utility providers could consider:
Time-of-use pricing to incentivize load shifting away from peak hours
Targeted demand-response programs during winter evenings to reduce grid stress
Infrastructure planning that accounts for heating-driven demand rather than assuming cooling-driven summer peaks

📁 Repository Structure
├── Delhi_Power_Consumption_Analysis.ipynb   # Full pipeline notebook
